(Q3) Explain with an example how the tee command can be used in logging.
     1. Real-time Monitoring
You can watch the process execute while simultaneously creating a permanent record. This is crucial for:
    • Long-running operations (backups, installations) 
    • Debugging issues as they occur 
    • Monitoring system health 
     2. Error Capture
Using 2>&1 | tee captures both stdout and stderr, ensuring you don't miss error messages
     3. Append vs Overwrite
    • tee file.log - Overwrites existing log (new session) 
    • tee -a file.log - Appends to existing log (continuous logging) 
code:
#!/bin/bash 
# backup_script.sh 
echo "Starting backup process at $(date)" 
cp -r /home/user/documents /backup/ 2>&1 | tee -a /var/log/backup.log 
echo "Backup completed at $(date)" 
(Q4) List the steps involved in installing Ubuntu 25.04 LTS on Oracle VirtualBox.
Part 1: Setting Up the Virtual Machine
Step 1: Create New Virtual Machine
    1. Open Oracle VirtualBox 
    2. Click "New" button 
    3. Enter VM details: 
        ◦ Name: Ubuntu 25.04 
        ◦ Type: Linux 
        ◦ Version: Ubuntu (64-bit) 
    4. Click "Next" 
Step 2: Configure Memory (RAM)
    1. Set memory size: 
        ◦ Minimum: 2048 MB (2GB) 
        ◦ Recommended: 4096 MB (4GB) or higher 
    2. Click "Next" 
Step 3: Create Virtual Hard Disk
    1. Select "Create a virtual hard disk now" 
    2. Click "Create" 
    3. Choose hard disk file type: 
        ◦ Select "VDI (VirtualBox Disk Image)" 
    4. Click "Next" 
    5. Storage allocation: 
        ◦ Select "Dynamically allocated" (recommended) 
    6. Click "Next" 
    7. Set disk size: 
        ◦ Minimum: 25 GB 
        ◦ Recommended: 50 GB or more 
    8. Click "Create" 
Step 4: Configure VM Settings
    1. Right-click on the new VM → "Settings" 
    2. System tab: 
        ◦ Processor: Allocate 2+ CPU cores 
        ◦ Enable "Enable PAE/NX" 
    3. Display tab: 
        ◦ Video Memory: 128 MB 
        ◦ Enable "3D Acceleration" 
    4. Storage tab: 
        ◦ Click on "Empty" CD icon 
        ◦ Click disk icon → "Choose Virtual Optical Disk File" 
        ◦ Select your Ubuntu 25.04 ISO file 
    5. Network tab: 
        ◦ Ensure "NAT" is selected (default) 
    6. Click "OK" 
Part 2: Installing Ubuntu 25.04
Step 5: Start the Virtual Machine
    1. Select your Ubuntu VM 
    2. Click "Start" 
    3. The VM will boot from the ISO file 
Step 6: Ubuntu Boot Menu
    1. Select "Try or Install Ubuntu" 
    2. Wait for Ubuntu to load (may take a few minutes) 
Step 7: Choose Installation Language
    1. Select your preferred language (English recommended) 
    2. Click "Continue" 
Step 8: Keyboard Layout
    1. Choose your keyboard layout 
    2. Test typing to verify layout 
    3. Click "Continue" 
Step 9: Updates and Other Software
    1. What apps would you like to install? 
        ◦ Select "Normal installation" (recommended) 
        ◦ Or "Minimal installation" for lighter setup 
    2. Other options: 
        ◦ Check "Download updates while installing Ubuntu" 
        ◦  Check "Install third-party software" (for graphics, Wi-Fi, etc.) 
    3. Click "Continue" 
Step 10: Installation Type
    1. Select "Erase disk and install Ubuntu" 
        ◦ Don't worry - this only affects the virtual disk 
    2. Click "Install Now" 
    3. Confirm by clicking "Continue" on the popup 
Step 11: Geographic Location
    1. Select your location on the map or from dropdown 
    2. This sets your timezone 
    3. Click "Continue" 
Step 12: User Account Setup
    1. Your name: Enter your full name 
    2. Your computer's name: Choose a hostname 
    3. Pick a username: Create a username (lowercase) 
    4. Choose a password: Enter a secure password 
    5. Confirm password: Re-enter the password 
    6. Login options: 
        ◦ "Require my password to log in" (recommended) 
        ◦ Or "Log in automatically" 
    7. Click "Continue" 
Step 13: Installation Process
    1. Ubuntu will now install (15-30 minutes) 
    2. You'll see a slideshow explaining Ubuntu features 
    3. Wait for "Installation Complete" message 
    4. Click "Restart Now" 
Step 14: First Boot
    1. The VM will restart 
    2. Remove installation medium prompt: Press Enter 
    3. Ubuntu will boot to login screen 
    4. Enter your username and password 
       sudo reboot
(Q5) During Ubuntu OS installation, you face a Kernel Panic Error. How would you troubleshoot it? 
     Step-by-Step Troubleshooting
Step 1: Immediate Actions
A. Reboot and Retry
    1. Hard reboot the system (power button) 
    2. Try again - sometimes it's a temporary glitch 
    3. If panic occurs at same point, proceed to next steps 
B. Record Error Details
    1. Take photo of error message if possible 
    2. Note the exact point where panic occurs: 
        ◦ During boot loader? 
        ◦ Loading kernel? 
        ◦ Hardware detection? 
        ◦ Mounting filesystems? 
Step 2: Boot Options and Kernel Parameters
A. Access GRUB Boot Menu
    1. Boot from Ubuntu ISO 
    2. Highlight "Try or Install Ubuntu" 
    3. Press 'e' to edit boot parameters 
    4. Find line starting with "linux" 
B. Add Troubleshooting Parameters
Add these parameters to the linux line (before "quiet splash"):
 # Basic troubleshooting options
acpi=off noapic nomodeset

# Memory-related issues
mem=4G memtest

# Graphics-related issues
nomodeset nouveau.modeset=0

# Disable problematic features
noapic nolapic nosmp

# Debug options
debug ignore_loglevel

# Complete example line:
linux /casper/vmlinuz file=/cdrom/preseed/ubuntu.seed boot=casper acpi=off nomodeset ---
       
